{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Seattle Mariners arrived with cautious optimism, and the season's very first game set the tone perfectly — a dramatic walk-off fielder's choice to steal a win, immediately followed by a brutal blown save on Opening Day that reminded everyone this team still had rough edges. Through April and May, the Mariners played competitive but inconsistent baseball, finishing just above .500 in April before dipping below in May. The bullpen — particularly Rafael Montero, who appeared on the wrong side of multiple high-leverage disasters — was a recurring source of heartbreak, costing Seattle games it had no business losing.\n\nJune and July represented the Mariners' most sustained stretch of winning baseball, with the team going 15-12 and 14-10 respectively. Unlikely heroes emerged: Shed Long Jr. cracked a clutch double against Tampa Bay, Taylor Trammell launched a big home run off the bench, and Dylan Moore delivered a stunning grand slam in late July that encapsulated the scrappy, unpredictable nature of this roster. The offense was far from dominant — a .688 OPS told that story clearly — but the team found ways to manufacture runs and grind out victories, building belief that a playoff appearance was genuinely possible.\n\nAugust brought turbulence. The Rangers, Yankees, and Athletics all took turns roughing up the Seattle bullpen in sequences that stung deeply. Erik Swanson and Diego Castillo surrendered leads that should have been protected, and a brutal back-to-back series against New York in early August threatened to derail everything. Yet the Mariners steadied themselves, going 15-13 for the month — ugly by their September standards, but enough to stay in the hunt. Ty France's two-run homer and Jake Bauers' clutch single in late August were reminders that this team had real fight in it.\n\nSeptember was when the 2021 Mariners truly revealed their character, going an extraordinary 18-8 to finish the regular season. Mitch Haniger — back from years of injury heartbreak — launched a three-run homer on September 13th that felt like a statement. José Marmolejos contributed a clutch two-RBI single to keep the wild card flame burning. The team won 90 games for the first time since 2003, ending the longest playoff drought in North American professional sports at 20 years — almost. A pair of crushing late-season blown saves by Paul Sewald, including a gut-punch three-run homer by Jared Walsh on the final weekend against the Angels, ultimately left Seattle one agonizing step outside the postseason. The 2021 Mariners were a promise kept to themselves, and a promise to their fans still waiting for October.",
